      "rationale": "If pendulum_obs possesses a biased or nonlinear response function (asymmetric hysteresis, rectification, or saturation at lower measurement range), and if the true pendulum oscillates in response to environmental forcing at 3–7 day quasi-period (as suggested by pendulum_lab and resonator_obs correlations), then pendulum_obs would integrate the oscillation nonlinearly and exhibit net downward drift while other streams show oscillation. This mechanistically explains why pendulum_obs drifts persistently (z = −2.21) while remaining decorrelated from the 0.820 correlation between lab instruments. However, this requires two independent causes to align: (1) nonlinearity specific to pendulum_obs response curve, and (2) environmental forcing driving true pendulum physics. Occam's Razor disfavors this mechanism because simpler single-cause explanations (calibration drift alone, or environmental forcing alone) account for the observed data pattern. Today's data provide no new evidence to test nonlinearity directly (no deliberate range excursion, no response-curve measurement, no temperature or environmental parameter log). Probability lowered from 0.03 to 0.04 because the consistency of pendulum_obs drift over 40 days without any detectable oscillatory component (events 26165, 257072, 750266, 957527, 185255, 220892, 768077, 824656, 584770, 82176, 322608, 888393, 26182, 290935, 372069, 111027) makes it less likely that a hidden oscillation is being rectified; a rectified oscillation should produce a modulated drift pattern rather than nearly linear decline. Hypothesis retained at 0.04 only to prompt future measurement of pendulum_obs response curve across full range and environmental parameters (temperature, humidity, pressure, acceleration) in independent test.",
