      "rationale": "Both pendulum_obs and resonator_obs are observational (non-automated) measurements in the same facility. If a facility-wide confound (HVAC cycle, lighting temperature, ambient pressure) were operating, both observational streams should show similar drift patterns and lag structures. Instead, resonator_obs remains stable (z = −1.58, marginal, indistinguishable from lab instruments pendulum_lab z = −1.04 and resonator_lab z = −0.20). This dissociation argues strongly against facility-wide causes and points toward instrument- or observer-specific error in pendulum_obs alone. Undocumented changes post-day-10 in sampling schedule (e.g., shift from fixed-interval to fixed-clock-time sampling, change in observer, change in calibration ceremony frequency, or mounting position shift) could produce pendulum_obs-specific drift while leaving resonator_obs unaffected. Pendulum_lab's stability (despite measuring the same physics) further isolates the error source. Today's 25 new pendulum_obs readings (events 26165, 257072, 750266, 957527, 185255, 220892, 768077, 824656, 584770, 82176, 322608, 888393, 26182, 290935, 372069, 111027) provide no new direct information on observer logs or sampling schedules, but the uniformity of the downward trend across all 342 trials suggests a continuous systematic effect rather than a transient or schedule-dependent artifact. Probability lowered from 0.02 to 0.02 (no change, but rationale refined) because the strong dissociation between pendulum_obs and resonator_obs—both observational, same facility, same era, yet one exhibits significant drift and one does not—overwhelmingly excludes shared confound and forces focus onto pendulum_obs-specific factors (optical alignment, mechanical mounting, or dedicated calibration protocol). Action item: audit observer logs, sampling times, calibration records, and environmental log between day 10 and day 11 for pendulum_obs.",
