  "summaryOfChange": "The central question has been refined to address the most critical anomaly: the dissociation between the strong 3-day-lagged correlation in lab instruments (pendulum_lab ↔ resonator_obs r = 0.820) and the complete decorrelation of pendulum_obs from both lab streams, despite pendulum_obs measuring the same pendulum physics as pendulum_lab. This refocuses attention from the isolated drift in pendulum_obs (which was the question in the prior review) to the broader puzzle of why two physically distinct instruments in the same lab exhibit synchronized variation that does not propagate to the third measurement stream of the same apparatus. The hypothesis set has been reorganized around this central question: Hypothesis 1 (now p=0.58, up from p=0.51) now explicitly posits a two-process model in which environmental forcing drives the 3–7 day cycle observed in lab instruments, while pendulum_obs contamination by independent systematic error masks its participation in that cycle—this is the most parsimonious explanation of the data and is strengthened by today's 25 new pendulum_obs readings, which extend the drift trend without any hint of phase locking to a 3–7 day oscillation. Hypothesis 2 (now p=0.07, down from p=0.08) skeptically acknowledges that the pendulum_lab ↔ resonator_obs correlation could be a multiple-comparisons artifact, but the consistency of lag structure across individual streams and the large total sample size (968 trial-days) lower this probability. Hypotheses 3 and 4 are retained at low probability (0.04 and 0.02) as reminders to pursue specific tests (nonlinearity measurement, observer log audit) but are not prioritized pending those tests. The residual probability (0.29, unchanged) acknowledges major unexplored avenues: facility event logs, electromagnetic environment assessment, oscilloscope traces, mechanical coupling survey, environmental sensor data, and resonance characterization—all of which could shift probability mass substantially if negative or positive findings emerge. No new major evidence appeared today (25 trials all consistent with prior trend); today's data strengthen the case for Hypothesis 1 by extending the observed drift duration and eliminating the possibility of a brief transient in pendulum_obs."
